<interrogationes unitas="arbor2_lexema">

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I use arbor2_lexema to tokenize C for a new tool?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexator_creare" via="project-specs/generationes-praecedentes-archivandae.md silva/CLAUDE.md lib/arbor2_expandere.c lib/lapifex_c89_grammatica.c">You
      can, but it is a predecessor generation: arbor v1, then arbor2
      (the hand-written GLR this lexer feeds), then lapifex, then
      silva, the current C parser with byte-exact round trips and the
      house tooling around it. Its remaining callers outside its own
      tests are arbor2_expandere, lapifex_c89_grammatica (lapifex in
      its retired C89-parser role) and the unbuilt tools/glr_debug.c.
      New C tooling should go through silva.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I tokenize a C source buffer with arbor2?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexator_creare arbor2_lexema_proximum arbor2_lexema_omnia arbor2_lexema_genus_nomen" via="lib/lapifex_c89_grammatica.c">Create
      a lexer with arbor2_lexator_creare(piscina, intern, fons,
      mensura), then either pull tokens one at a time with
      arbor2_lexema_proximum until genus is ARBOR2_LEXEMA_EOF, or call
      arbor2_lexema_omnia for everything at once. Unlike html_lexare,
      omnia returns a Xar of Arbor2Lexema POINTERS: read each element
      as *(Arbor2Lexema**)xar_obtinere(x, i). arbor2_lexema_genus_nomen
      names a genus for debugging ("IDENTIFICATOR", "SEMICOLON",
      ...).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>where do whitespace, comments and newlines go?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexema_proximum Arbor2Lexema" via="lib/arbor2_lexema.worklog.md">They
      never appear in the main stream. They become typed Arbor2Lexema
      tokens (SPATIA, TABULAE, CONTINUATIO, COMMENTUM_CLAUSUM,
      COMMENTUM_LINEA, NOVA_LINEA) in a token's spatia_ante or
      spatia_post Xar (Xar of Arbor2Lexema*, NIHIL when empty).
      Trailing trivia on the same line, up to and including the first
      newline, goes to spatia_post of the preceding token; everything
      after that goes to spatia_ante of the next token. The header
      still says newlines are NOVA_LINEA tokens in the stream, but
      since Phase 2.7 they live only in spatia.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the lexer read code written with latina.h keywords?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexema_proximum">Yes.
      The keyword table holds both the C89 keywords and their
      latina.h spellings, so 'si' lexes as ARBOR2_LEXEMA_IF, 'nomen'
      as TYPEDEF, 'redde' as RETURN, and 'staticus', 'interior',
      'hic_manens' and 'universalis' all as STATIC. Other latina.h
      macros (NIHIL, VERUM, the Roman numerals, imprimere) are plain
      identifiers. The lexer never produces NOMEN_TYPUS or
      TYPEDEF_MACRO; typedef names are remapped later by the
      parser.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens when the source contains a character C does not use, like @ or $?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexema_omnia arbor2_lexema_proximum">arbor2_lexema_proximum
      consumes one byte and returns an ARBOR2_LEXEMA_ERROR token. But
      arbor2_lexema_omnia STOPS at the first ERROR token: for "a @ b"
      it returns IDENTIFICATOR a and ERROR, with no b and no EOF. Any
      non-ASCII byte (such as UTF-8 outside a string or comment) does
      the same. Pull with arbor2_lexema_proximum if you need to keep
      going past an error.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free the source buffer once I have the tokens?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexator_creare arbor2_lexema_omnia Arbor2Lexema">Yes,
      after the last token is read: every valor (and every trivia
      token's valor) is copied into the InternamentumChorda you passed,
      so it lives as long as that intern's piscina, not the source.
      The lexer itself keeps pointing into fons, so keep the buffer
      while you are still calling arbor2_lexema_proximum. valor is a
      chorda and is not NUL-terminated; byte_offset and longitudo
      locate the token in the original source.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what number and string forms does the lexer recognize?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor2_lexema_proximum">Decimal,
      0x hex and 0-octal integers with U, L, UL, LL, ULL and LU
      suffixes (either case, LLU too), and decimal floats with a
      fraction or exponent and an optional f/F/l/L suffix. A float
      must start with a digit and have a digit after the point: '.5'
      lexes as PUNCTUM then INTEGER, '1.' (and '1.e5') as INTEGER then
      PUNCTUM, and an octal-looking '012.5' as INTEGER 012, PUNCTUM,
      INTEGER 5. There is no error for these splits. An unterminated string or character
      literal ends at the newline and is still returned as STRING_LIT
      or CHAR_LIT, with no error token.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what do linea, columna and standard mean on a token?
    <responsum symbolum="Arbor2Lexema arbor2_lexema_proximum">linea
      and columna are 1-based and count bytes; byte_offset is 0-based.
      A \r\n or a bare \r is one NOVA_LINEA, but only \n advances
      linea: after a bare \r the next token still reports the same
      line. One known defect: a //
      comment that lands in spatia_ante (a comment on its own line)
      includes its newline and advances linea twice, so tokens after
      it report a line one too high. standard is always
      ARBOR2_STANDARD_C89 from this lexer, even for // comments.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
